{"name":"Peat Brown","hex":"#5a3d29","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"peat","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Soil formed of dead but not fully decayed plants found in bog areas, often burned as fuel. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A pet, a darling; a woman."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/peat"},{"term":"brown","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour like that of chocolate or coffee.","One of the colour balls used in snooker, with a value of 4 points."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a brown color/colour.","Gloomy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brown"}],"entries":[{"title":"Peat","blocks":[{"text":"Peat is an accumulation of partially decayed vegetation or organic matter. It is unique to natural areas called peatlands, bogs, mires, moors, or muskegs. Sphagnum moss, also called peat moss, is one of the most common components in peat, although many other plants can contribute. The biological features of sphagnum mosses act to create a habitat aiding peat formation, a phenomenon termed 'habitat manipulation'. Soils consisting primarily of peat are known as histosols. Peat forms in wetland conditions, where flooding or stagnant water obstructs the flow of oxygen from the atmosphere, slowing the rate of decomposition. Peat properties such as organic matter content and saturated hydraulic conductivity can exhibit high spatial heterogeneity."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Peat_%2849302157252%29.jpg/330px-Peat_%2849302157252%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peat","wikidata":"Q184624"},{"title":"Brown","blocks":[{"text":"Brown is a color. It can be thought as a darker, typically desaturated shade of orange, and can often be produced by combining red and yellow with another color, namely blue."}],"image":"htt